toolchain, and can a second workflow reach the evidence channel
run 2 - 2026-09-01 - run 1 died on "pwsh: command not found": the PATH
bootstrap had been copied without its pwsh half. Complete block restored.
run 3 - 2026-09-01 - run 2 measured correctly but shipped an empty sha on
line 1; provenance now reads GITHUB_SHA. Re-run to confirm the fix.
run 4 - 2026-09-01 - FIRST BUILD ATTEMPT. Compiles the ported perception core
as a UE Program target, times a cold and a warm build separately, and runs
the 1221-row golden test. Written to be informative when it fails: the exact
UBT invocation for a project-hosted Program target in 5.8 is not testable
from the container, so the step captures the command, the exit code, the
elapsed time and the log tail either way.
run 5 - 2026-09-01 - build attempt 1 failed in 44s with "Targets with a
unique build environment cannot be built with an installed engine". The
target overrode five engine-wide settings; removed, and BuildEnvironment is
now Shared explicitly. An installed engine is the configuration Jafar has and
therefore the one D1 must measure.
run 6 - 2026-09-01 - build attempt 2 failed in 2.72s with "Program targets
are not currently supported from this engine distribution". Not a setting: an
installed engine cannot build a Program target at all. Converted to a GAME
target, which is what LEDGER would be and therefore the cycle worth pricing;
the golden test moved into the primary game module's startup and writes
golden-result.txt, which the step reads. Also raised the job cap above the
build step's cap (25 vs 60 would have killed a legitimate cold build and
reported it as NO PROBE) and made the binary pick newest-first with a
candidate count. Expect the cold build to take real minutes for the first
time: the engine is genuinely linked now, and that is the measurement.
run 7 - 2026-09-01 - run 6 BUILT: cold 1.12 min, warm 0.03, 324 MB binary.
The toolchain question is closed. The binary then exited 3 in six seconds
having written nothing, and the step could say nothing about why. This run
is about that: the game's stdout and stderr are captured and tailed,
LedgerProbe.log is tailed by name, the module writes a moduleStartup
breadcrumb before any work so "never loaded" and "loaded and died" separate,
and the project gets the minimum Config it was missing (UBT does not need
one, the runtime does). Cold is also MADE cold now by clearing Intermediate
and Binaries first, because run 6 was the last run that would have been
honestly cold by accident.
run 8 - 2026-09-01 - the diagnosis channel worked and named the fault
exactly. Run 7 built clean and then died in the async package loader:
"Failed to load premade asset registry", then an assertion in BufferReader
on an IO thread. Build.bat compiles code; it does not cook content, and an
uncooked game binary crashes reading packages that were never produced.
Nothing was wrong with the C++. So this run cooks: RunUAT BuildCookRun,
cold and incremental timed separately, the packaged binary preferred for
the test because it is the one with content beside it, and the golden table
copied next to it because a staged build's ProjectDir is the staged tree.
The cook time is not a workaround, it is the half of the UE cycle that
Build.bat was never measuring.
run 9 - 2026-09-01 - the cook died in two seconds on "Editor target not
found!". Cooking is not a build step in Unreal, it is a commandlet run
inside the editor, so UAT refuses before it starts unless the project
declares an editor target; the project had only a game target. Added, with
the same Shared build environment for the same installed-engine reason.
-nocompileeditor is dropped with it: a C++ project cannot be cooked without
its editor module built, and that compile is real UE setup cost that
belongs in the cold cook number rather than being hidden by a flag that
makes the cook fail instead. Expect the cold cook to be the longest step
this probe has run.
run 10 - 2026-09-01 - the .NET Framework SDK is in. The fixed setup guard
found 2 of 3 required components missing, modified the existing Build Tools
install in place, and verified 3 of 3 in 0.4 minutes; the old guard would
have reported ALREADY PRESENT and changed nothing. Nothing in the probe
changed for this run. It is the same cook, on a machine that can now build
an editor target, and it is the first run with a chance of producing a cook
number at all.
run 11 - 2026-09-01 - run 10 got the editor BUILT and the cook actually
running: 32 seconds inside UnrealEditor-Cmd before Error_UnknownCookFailure.
The reason was above the 40-line tail, which is the cap-biting-silently
fault in my own instrument, so error lines are now pulled from the whole
cook log with both caps announced. Two hypotheses ride this run. The cook
has no content to cook, so MapsToCook points at an engine map that
certainly exists, which tests the cook path without inventing a binary
asset. And the game module never reaches StartupModule at all: the
breadcrumb has come back absent on every run since it was added, so the
package-loader crash happens BEFORE Default-phase module loading. The
module moves to PostConfigInit. That is a deliberate gamble: UE may refuse
a non-Default phase for a primary game module, and if it does it will say
so plainly and cost one round trip.
